# ── Feminist standpoint theory ──
card("feminist-standpoint-theory", "Feminist standpoint theory",
  V("W2078186038", "Record dated 2016 (later printing of the 1991 book)."),
  "Knowledge is shaped by the social position of those who produce it. Starting research from the lives of marginalized groups, such as women, can reveal aspects of social relations that are invisible from dominant positions. Harding argues this yields 'strong objectivity': more objective knowledge, because researchers examine their own social location instead of claiming a view from nowhere.",
  ["How do women or other marginalized groups experience and understand an institution, a policy or a workplace?",
   "Whose knowledge counts in a field, and what is missed when research starts from dominant groups?",
   "Typical data: in-depth interviews, life histories, institutional ethnography, participatory methods."],
  ["Claiming that being a woman automatically gives better knowledge. A standpoint is achieved through critical reflection, not given by identity.",
   "Treating 'women' as one group without attention to race, class, ethnicity or location.",
   "Using the term without addressing your own position as researcher."],
  [("foundation", V("W4242724764", "2003 reprint of the 1983 chapter."), "Grounds a feminist standpoint in women's material, everyday activity, by analogy with Marxist analysis of labour."),
   ("foundation", V("W2061732992"), "Black women's 'outsider within' position as a distinctive source of sociological insight."),
   ("refinement", V("W3146083582"), "Situated knowledges: objectivity as partial, located and accountable perspective.")],
  [("Black feminist thought", "Knowledge from the intersecting positions of race, gender and class.", V("W2061732992")),
   ("Situated knowledges", "All knowledge is partial and located; objectivity means being accountable for one's location.", V("W3146083582"))],
  [("Gives researchers a principled argument for starting from marginalized lives and for reflecting on their own position.", V("W2078186038", "Record dated 2016 (later printing of the 1991 book).")),
   ("Has been clarified and refined over decades and moved closer to feminist empiricism in useful ways.", V("W2150575490"))],
  [("Struggles to justify the epistemic privilege of any one standpoint once multiple, intersecting standpoints are recognized.", V("W2000460168")),
   ("The line between standpoint theory and feminist empiricism has become unclear.", V("W2150575490"))],
  [("original", V("W2078186038", "Record dated 2016 (later printing of the 1991 book)."), None, "Where 'strong objectivity' is developed.", "What does your research see if it starts from the lives of the least powerful people in your setting?"),
   ("refinement", V("W3146083582"), None, "A widely cited essay on objectivity as situated knowledge.", "How will you account for your own position in this research?"),
   ("review", V("W2150575490"), None, "Where the debate stands after 25 years.", "Is your approach closer to standpoint theory or to feminist empiricism, and why?")],
  notes="Smith (1987), Harding (1992, 2004) and Longino (1993) had no clean records and are left out.")

# ── Norm localization ──
card("norm-localization", "Norm Localization",
  V("W2110438916"),
  "International norms do not simply diffuse from global to local. Local actors actively select, reinterpret and graft external norms onto existing local beliefs and practices, so that the norm fits local identity and legitimacy. Acharya shows this with Southeast Asian regionalism: norms are more likely to take hold when they can be localized.",
  ["How was an international norm or policy (human rights, gender equality, anti-corruption, education standards) adopted, adapted or resisted in a country or region?",
   "What role do local actors and traditions play in shaping how global norms are used?",
   "Typical data: policy documents, interviews with officials and civil society, process tracing, comparative case studies."],
  ["Treating any partial adoption as 'localization'. Localization is active reconstruction by local actors, not just incomplete compliance.",
   "Ignoring the local norms and prior beliefs the external norm is grafted onto.",
   "Assuming localization is always positive for the norm's original purpose."],
  [("foundation", V("W2164304100"), "The norm life cycle: emergence, cascade, internalization."),
   ("foundation", V("W2029732602"), "Domestic structures and identity shape whether international norms take hold."),
   ("refinement", V("W1738564968"), "Norm subsidiarity: local actors create their own rules to resist or check powerful outsiders."),
   ("refinement", V("W1985595088"), "Post-conflict states show outcomes between resistance, compliance and localization.")],
  [("Norm contestation", "Norms are contested in use; contested compliance reveals changes in their meaning.", V("W3126073030")),
   ("Norm subsidiarity", "Local rule-making to resist or check outside powers.", V("W1738564968"))],
  [("Gives local actors agency in norm diffusion, instead of seeing them as passive receivers.", V("W2110438916")),
   ("Explains uneven responses to global norms across regional organizations.", V("W2085873866"))],
  [("Localization can work as resistance: a regional body may mediate global norms in ways that dilute action.", V("W2085873866")),
   ("Established diffusion approaches, including localization, miss outcomes observed in post-conflict states.", V("W1985595088"))],
  [("original", V("W2110438916"), None, "The founding article; clear and case-based.", "Which local norms or beliefs could an external norm be grafted onto in your case?"),
   ("refinement", V("W2164304100"), None, "The model localization argues against; read to understand the debate.", "Where in the norm life cycle does your case sit, and where does it not fit?"),
   ("review", V("W1985595088"), None, "Shows the range of outcomes and how to tell them apart.", "Which outcome (resistance, compliance, localization) does your evidence point to?")],
  notes="Zwingel (2012) and Epstein (2012) had no clean records and are left out.")

# ── Resource-based view ──
card("resource-based-view", "Resource-Based View",
  V("W3124536214"),
  "Firms differ in the resources they control, and those differences persist. A resource can be a source of sustained competitive advantage if it is valuable, rare, hard to imitate and non-substitutable (the VRIN criteria). Strategy should therefore start from what the firm has, not only from the industry it is in.",
  ["Why do some firms outperform others in the same industry?",
   "Which resources or capabilities explain a firm's or sector's advantage (e.g. in SMEs, family firms, startups)?",
   "Typical data: firm surveys, financial data, case studies of resources and capabilities."],
  ["Calling any asset a 'resource' without showing it meets the VRIN criteria.",
   "Arguing in a circle: the resource is valuable because the firm succeeded.",
   "Ignoring how resources are built and changed over time (see dynamic capabilities)."],
  [("foundation", V("W2140956556"), "Looks at firms in terms of their resources rather than their products."),
   ("refinement", V("W2101946146"), "Resources are stocks built up over time, which makes them hard to imitate."),
   ("refinement", V("W2122679534"), "Four conditions for competitive advantage: heterogeneity, ex post and ex ante limits to competition, imperfect mobility.")],
  [("Dynamic capabilities", "How firms build and reconfigure resources in changing environments (own library entry).", V("W2123709218")),
   ("Knowledge-based view", "Knowledge as the most strategic resource (own library entry).", V("W2160449301"))],
  [("Has grown into one of the most prominent theories for understanding organizations.", V("W2137945789")),
   ("Its core message withstands most critiques if variables, boundaries and applicability are specified.", V("W2198189408"))],
  [("Not yet a theory: definitions are imprecise, product markets are assumed stable and causality stays in a 'black box'.", V("W2043189549")),
   ("Some critiques (e.g. on the definition of resources and value) remain serious.", V("W2198189408"))],
  [("original", V("W3124536214"), None, "The founding statement of the VRIN logic.", "Which resource in your case might meet all four criteria, and how will you show it?"),
   ("refinement", V("W2122679534"), None, "A clear model of the conditions for advantage.", "Which of Peteraf's conditions hold in your case?"),
   ("review", V("W2198189408"), None, "Maps the eight main critiques and what survives them.", "Which critique applies most to your study, and how will you handle it?")],
  notes="Penrose (1959) had no clean record and is left out.")

# ── Self-determination theory ──
card("self-determination-theory", "Self-determination theory",
  V("W2052729098"),
  "People have three basic psychological needs: autonomy, competence and relatedness. When environments support these needs, people are more intrinsically motivated, internalize goals more fully and do better. Motivation is not just 'more or less' but differs in quality, from controlled (pressure, rewards) to autonomous (interest, values).",
  ["What supports or undermines students', employees' or patients' motivation?",
   "How do teaching, management or parenting styles relate to autonomy, competence and relatedness?",
   "Typical data: validated scales (need satisfaction, motivation types), experiments, interviews."],
  ["Treating motivation as a single amount instead of distinguishing types (intrinsic, identified, introjected, external).",
   "Equating autonomy with independence. Autonomy means acting with volition, which can include relying on others.",
   "Assuming rewards always undermine intrinsic motivation; effects depend on how rewards are given."],
  [("refinement", V("W4242883546"), "The widely cited summary: basic needs and their link to motivation, development and well-being."),
   ("refinement", V("W2122517769"), "The 'what' and 'why' of goals: need satisfaction and the regulation of behaviour."),
   ("refinement", V("W2007851141"), "Meta-analysis: tangible, expected rewards tend to undermine intrinsic motivation."),
   ("refinement", V("W2084716978"), "Need satisfaction and frustration matter across four cultures.")],
  [("Work motivation", "Applications of the theory in organizations.", V("W2084389751"))],
  [("Need satisfaction and frustration relate to well-being across cultures, not only in the West.", V("W2084716978")),
   ("A broad framework that links motivation, development and well-being.", V("W4242883546"))],
  [("Rewards do not, overall, decrease intrinsic motivation; verbal praise can increase it.", V("W4253356459")),
   ("Personal choice enhanced motivation more for American than for Asian participants, questioning the universal value of choice.", V("W2114883239"))],
  [("original", V("W2052729098"), None, "The founding book; the later articles are easier to start with.", "Which of the three needs is most at stake in your setting?"),
   ("refinement", V("W4242883546"), None, "The most cited short overview.", "Which types of motivation will you distinguish, and how will you measure them?"),
   ("review", V("W2114883239"), None, "The key cultural challenge to the theory.", "Does your cultural setting change how autonomy or choice works?")],
  notes="Ryan & Deci (2017) had no clean record and is left out.")

