# ── Constructivist learning theory ──
card("constructivist-learning-theory", "Constructivist learning theory",
  V("W2135943618"),
  "Learners build knowledge actively rather than receiving it. Piaget focused on how individuals construct understanding through interaction with the world; Vygotsky stressed that learning is social and mediated by language and tools, and that learners can do more with help than alone (the zone of proximal development).",
  ["How do students build understanding in a course, programme or learning technology?",
   "How do peer interaction, scaffolding or tools support learning?",
   "Typical data: classroom observation, interviews, learning artefacts, design-based research."],
  ["Equating constructivism with unguided discovery learning.",
   "Mixing Piaget's individual and Vygotsky's social constructivism without saying which you use.",
   "Treating it as a teaching method rather than a theory of learning."],
  [("foundation", V("W2157904933"), "How children construct intelligence through action and adaptation."),
   ("refinement", V("W1970209023"), "Scaffolding: how a tutor supports a learner to do what they could not do alone."),
   ("refinement", V("W2164599981"), "Situated cognition: knowledge is tied to the activity and culture in which it is learned.")],
  [("Social constructivism", "Learning through interaction and cultural tools.", V("W2102531419")),
   ("Situated learning", "Learning as participation in authentic activity.", V("W2164599981"))],
  [("A rich account of how learning happens through social interaction and tools.", V("W2102531419")),
   ("Explains why knowledge learned out of context often fails to transfer.", V("W2164599981"))],
  [("Minimally guided instruction based on constructivist ideas is less effective than guided instruction, given human cognitive architecture.", V("W1976637107")),
   ("Pure discovery learning does not work; constructivist learning needs guidance.", V("W131038430")),
   ("'Constructivism' covers many different positions, some sound and some not.", V("W2096126412"))],
  [("original", V("W2135943618"), "Chapter 6, 'Interaction between learning and development'", "Where the zone of proximal development is introduced.", "What can your learners do with help that they cannot yet do alone?"),
   ("refinement", V("W1970209023"), None, "The source of the term 'scaffolding'.", "What forms of scaffolding exist in your setting?"),
   ("review", V("W1976637107"), None, "The strongest critique, important for any study of instruction.", "How much guidance do learners get in your setting, and why does that matter?")])

# ── Dynamic capabilities ──
card("dynamic-capabilities", "Dynamic capabilities",
  V("W2123709218"),
  "In fast-changing environments, advantage comes less from the resources a firm holds and more from its ability to integrate, build and reconfigure them. Dynamic capabilities are these higher-order capacities: sensing opportunities, seizing them and transforming the organization.",
  ["How do firms adapt to technological or market change?",
   "Which routines or managerial capacities allow organizations to renew themselves?",
   "Typical data: longitudinal case studies, firm surveys, interviews with managers."],
  ["Calling any capability 'dynamic'. Dynamic capabilities change other capabilities.",
   "Defining the capability by its outcome (the firm adapted, so it had dynamic capabilities).",
   "Ignoring the difference between Teece's view (source of lasting advantage) and Eisenhardt & Martin's (best-practice routines)."],
  [("refinement", V("W2089402107"), "Dynamic capabilities as identifiable routines; in high-velocity markets they are simple and experiential."),
   ("refinement", V("W2018514036"), "Dynamic capabilities develop through experience, knowledge articulation and codification."),
   ("refinement", V("W2059813301"), "Distinguishes ordinary capabilities from dynamic ones, and ad hoc problem solving."),
   ("synthesis", V("W2169667133"), "Microfoundations: sensing, seizing and transforming.")],
  [("Entrepreneurship and dynamic capabilities", "How new and established firms build and use dynamic capabilities.", V("W1525360602"))],
  [("One of the most used theoretical lenses in management, with growing empirical knowledge.", V("W2767795466"))],
  [("The construct is underspecified and empirical knowledge has lagged behind the theory.", V("W2767795466")),
   ("The view adds little beyond existing theories and lacks clear foundations and evidence.", V("W2120828026"))],
  [("original", V("W2123709218"), None, "The founding article.", "Which capability in your case changes other capabilities?"),
   ("refinement", V("W2089402107"), None, "The main alternative view; compare it with Teece.", "Which of the two views fits your setting better?"),
   ("review", V("W2767795466"), None, "A content-analytic review of the field.", "Which open question in the review does your study address?")])

